NNAAMMEE
     mmrroouutteedd — IP multicast routing daemon

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     mmrroouutteedd [--pp] [--cc _c_o_n_f_i_g___f_i_l_e] [--dd[_d_e_b_u_g___l_e_v_e_l]]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     mmrroouutteedd is an implementation of the Distance-Vector Multicast Routing
     Protocol (DVMRP), an earlier version of which is specified in RFC 1075.
     It maintains topological knowledge via a distance-vector routing protocol
     (like RIP, described in RFC 1058), upon which it implements a multicast
     datagram forwarding algorithm called Reverse Path Multicasting.

     mmrroouutteedd forwards a multicast datagram along a shortest (reverse) path
     tree rooted at the subnet on which the datagram originates.  The multi‐
     cast delivery tree may be thought of as a broadcast delivery tree that
     has been pruned back so that it does not extend beyond those subnetworks
     that have members of the destination group.  Hence, datagrams are not
     forwarded along those branches which have no listeners of the multicast
     group.  The IP time-to-live of a multicast datagram can be used to limit
     the range of multicast datagrams.

     In order to support multicasting among subnets that are separated by
     (unicast) routers that do not support IP multicasting, mmrroouutteedd includes
     support for "tunnels", which are virtual point-to-point links between
     pairs of mmrroouutteedd daemons located anywhere in an internet.  IP multicast
     packets are encapsulated for transmission through tunnels, so that they
     look like normal unicast datagrams to intervening routers and subnets.
     The encapsulation is added on entry to a tunnel, and stripped off on exit
     from a tunnel.  By default, the packets are encapsulated using the IP-in-
     IP protocol (IP protocol number 4).  Older versions of mmrroouutteedd tunnel use
     IP source routing, which puts a heavy load on some types of routers.
     This version does not support IP source route tunnelling.

     The tunnelling mechanism allows mmrroouutteedd to establish a virtual internet,
     for the purpose of multicasting only, which is independent of the physi‐
     cal internet, and which may span multiple Autonomous Systems.  This capa‐
     bility is intended for experimental support of internet multicasting
     only, pending widespread support for multicast routing by the regular
     (unicast) routers.  mmrroouutteedd suffers from the well-known scaling problems
     of any distance-vector routing protocol, and does not (yet) support hier‐
     archical multicast routing.

     mmrroouutteedd handles multicast routing only; there may or may not be unicast
     routing software running on the same machine as mmrroouutteedd.  With the use of
     tunnels, it is not necessary for mmrroouutteedd to have access to more than one
     physical subnet in order to perform multicast forwarding.

     The options are as follows:

     --cc _c_o_n_f_i_g___f_i_l_e
             Specify an alternative configuration file, instead of the default
             _m_r_o_u_t_e_d_._c_o_n_f.

     --dd[_d_e_b_u_g___l_e_v_e_l]
             By default, mmrroouutteedd detaches from the invoking terminal.  If this
             option is specified, mmrroouutteedd remains attached to the invoking
             terminal and responsive to signals from that terminal.  If --dd is
             given with no argument, the debug level defaults to 2.  Regard‐
             less of the debug level, mmrroouutteedd always writes warning and error
             messages to the system log daemon.  Debug levels have the follow‐
             ing effects:

                   0       Detach from the invoking terminal.
                   1       All syslog(3) messages are also printed to stderr.
                   2       All level 1 messages plus notifications of "signif‐
                           icant" events are printed to stderr.
                   3       All level 2 messages plus notifications of all
                           packet arrivals and departures are printed to
                           stderr.

     --pp      Start mmrroouutteedd in a non-pruning mode.  It is expected that a
             router would be configured in this manner for test purposes only.
             The default mode is pruning enabled.

     mmrroouutteedd automatically configures itself to forward on all multicast-capa‐
     ble interfaces, i.e. interfaces that have the IFF_MULTICAST flag set (ex‐
     cluding the loopback "interface"), and it finds other mmrroouutteedd directly
     reachable via those interfaces.  To override the default configuration,
     or to add tunnel links to other mmrroouutteedd, configuration commands may be
     placed in _/_e_t_c_/_m_r_o_u_t_e_d_._c_o_n_f.  There are five types of configuration com‐
     mands:

